WEBVTT

1
00:00:03.400 --> 00:00:07.560
Clathrin is a protein shaped like a three-legged pinwheel.

2
00:00:08.280 --> 00:00:13.340
Many copies lock together into a curved cage on the inner face of the membrane.

3
00:00:14.200 --> 00:00:17.840
Yet clathrin touches neither the membrane nor the cargo.

4
00:00:18.500 --> 00:00:19.500
So what does?

5
00:00:20.383 --> 00:00:22.083
Adaptor proteins do.

6
00:00:22.883 --> 00:00:26.423
The main adaptor at the plasma membrane is AP2.

7
00:00:27.163 --> 00:00:33.283
It holds the lipid PIP2, a short sequence in a receptor tail, and clathrin.

8
00:00:34.123 --> 00:00:40.443
So a receptor enters a pit because its tail carries a sequence that an adaptor can read.

10
00:00:40.566 --> 00:00:45.226
Brown and Goldstein met the proof in a patient known as J.D.

11
00:00:46.126 --> 00:00:49.186
This patient's LDL receptors could bind LDL.

12
00:00:49.666 --> 00:00:54.646
But they stayed scattered over the surface instead of gathering in pits, and the

13
00:00:54.646 --> 00:00:56.746
cells could not take LDL in.

14
00:00:57.646 --> 00:01:03.966
The fault was later traced to one tyrosine in the receptor's tail, replaced by cysteine.

16
00:01:04.300 --> 00:01:08.720
A pit deepens until it hangs from the surface by a thin neck.

17
00:01:09.740 --> 00:01:14.620
Dynamin, a large GTPase, assembles into a collar around the neck.

18
00:01:15.420 --> 00:01:18.560
When it hydrolyzes GTP, the neck is cut.

19
00:01:19.500 --> 00:01:23.860
The coat is then stripped off, and the bare vesicle can fuse with its target.
